  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1254 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nA deep upper low near Hudson Bay has an elongated trough axis\nextending southwest into the northern and central Plains, with\nKansas under west to northwest flow aloft and strong high pressure\nbuilding over central Canada. At the surface, the cold front sat\nalong the Nebraska border late Tuesday evening and moves through\nbefore sunrise. Tuesday reached the low 70s again, the second\nstraight day above the NBM, but dewpoints held in the mid to upper\n40s with light winds along the washed out front near Interstate 70\ninstead of mixing out like Monday.\n\nNorthwest winds increase behind the front this morning as mixing\ndeepens to several thousand feet, tapping 30 to 40 kt winds near\nthe top of the mixed layer. The NBM came in low on gusts given\nthat much wind available to mix down, so gusts were adjusted\nupward, with 25 to 35 mph likely this afternoon, strongest where\nmixing runs deepest across north central Kansas, with highs in the\nmid 60s before the colder air arrives. Winds ease this evening as\nthe boundary layer decouples and the pressure gradient begins to\nrelax.\n\nAs the western ridge amplifies, another lobe of energy rotates\naround the back side of the Hudson Bay upper low Thursday and\nreinforces the cold advection, dropping H85 temperatures toward 0\nC by evening with highs struggling to reach 50. The Canadian high\nthen settles overhead Thursday night, and with clear skies and\ncalm winds, Friday morning sets up as an ideal radiational cooling\nnight. Lows in the mid to upper 20s bring a hard freeze area-wide,\nwith valley areas running a few degrees colder, and the NBM has\ntrended colder each of the last two nights. Saturday morning stays\ncold, though light southeast winds returning to north central\nKansas keep lows a few degrees warmer there. Frost and freeze\nproducts ended for the season November 1, but these two cold\nmornings likely end a growing season that has run longer this\nfall.\n\nA weak Omega type block sets up over central Canada, which forces\na southern stream low into the southern Plains Sunday, keeping\nmost of its precipitation south of the area. Only the north edge\nclips the area Sunday into Sunday night, mainly across central\nKansas. The GFS keeps the rain shield south with only a shallow\nmoist layer aloft, while the ECMWF brings deeper moisture into\ncentral Kansas, but both hold dry air below 5000 ft fed by the\nhigh to the northeast, so early rain may evaporate before reaching\nthe ground especially north of Interstate 70. Ensemble guidance\nkeeps the median amounts light, with a quarter of the clusters dry\naltogether, so the signal stays weak despite a few wetter members.\nWith that much spread between the deterministic models and\nensemble members, confidence in rain amounts and coverage remains\nlow at this range. Drier weather follows early next week with a\nslow warming trend.",
